A||N||18||Xtreme Fiero's||Choping my springs. ||||1||
Z||000000||Xtreme Fiero's||10-09-2000||07:29 PM||||I have a set of 84 SE "hard" springs from my parts car I want to chop and mut on my driver, I would like to go down 2-2.5" Front and back. how do I maessure what I cut? how much will 1 coil drop it? and do I take off the same amount for the front & back? has anyone else cut thier springs?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||rockcrawl||10-09-2000||08:47 PM||||Wow, thats a tough question! In order to really figure it out, you'd have to measure the distance between each coil with the weight of the car on them, add the length of the control arm, multiply by the number of cylinders, subtract the radius of the tire times pie squared.... or is it diameter.... and then divide by the desired drop and add rotor thickness.<BR> That should tell you how much to cut in furlongs, but I always just guess! Remember, measure twice, cut once!! Or in this case cut a little and if it's not enough, cut a little more.<P>But seriously, from what I recall, one full coil will take it down about 2" on the front. I don't remember about the rears, but I'd say maybe 1/2 coil for 2" drop. On the front, if the control arm is 16" long, and the spring is directly in the center, 8" from the ball joint and 8" from the pivot bushings, the spring would travel 1" for every 2" of wheel travel. So for a 2" drop, you'd need to remove 1" from the total length of the <B>compressed</B> coil. But thats only if the coil is at the center of the inner and outer pivots. You need to adjust the formula if it's not (and it's most likely not).<BR> For the rear, there is no mechanical advantage on the spring. If you want a 2" drop, you need to remove 2" from the total length of the <B>compressed</B> coil.<P>Best way to do it, have the car sitting normal, mark the springs at the desired drop from the top (taking into account the leverage ratio for the front), and then remove them and cut them leaving a little extra just in case.<P>Someone please correct me if I'm wrong!<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"<p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 10-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Spyder||10-09-2000||09:59 PM||||When you cut a half a coil it equals one inch.<BR>So one coil cut off will equal two inches.<BR>Depending on your springs it can very a 1/4”.<BR>While you are lowering the car back on the ground make sure the rear springs are in their top perches.<BR>When the car is back on the ground sit on or put pressure on each corner of the car to get the car to it’s new ride height. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Xtreme Fiero's||10-09-2000||10:05 PM||||Thanks Guys, I thin i'll go with 1 coil all the way around<P><BR>BUT how can 1 coil front & back be a level drop? the springs are WAY diffrent, Ther rears are even progressive do I still take 1 coil out all the way around?||||reg||1
Z||000004||007DOUG||10-10-2000||10:06 AM||||I just replaced all four springs on an 86 GT thats the previous owner had cut to lower the car.<P>It appears as if he cut 1 1/2 coils.<P>I wouldn't reccommend this, the ride was 100% horrible. Bottomed out alot.<P>Go with lowering springs. They are designed with a stiffer absorbing rate. <P>If you cut the originals, they aren't going to absorb the road very well.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Xtreme Fiero's||10-10-2000||10:24 AM||||<BR>007Doug: when they cut the springs they could have just torched them (soft ride), torched them & cold shocked them (a bit stiffer) or put the spring in a 5-gallon bucket of ice and cut wiht a cutoff wheel (Verry stiff ride).||||reg||1
Z||000006||2xsess||10-10-2000||11:17 AM||||What if you just cut them with a cutoff wheel?||||reg||5
Z||000007||Xtreme Fiero's||10-10-2000||11:41 AM||||<BR>2xsess: just a cutoff wheel would be better/stiffer than torching & cold shock but no as stiff as a cutoff wheel and cold shock. The cooler you keep the spring as you cut, the harder/firmer the ride. if you just torch them the srings will be slopy and sag later.||||reg||1
Z||000008||elfiero||10-10-2000||11:58 AM||||ON MY FRONT COILS,I USED A CUT OFF WHEEL AND<BR>REMOVED TWO COILS FROM THE "BOTTOM" OF THE<BR>SPRING.THIS LOWERED THE CAR SO YOU CAN JUST<BR>FIT YOUR THUMB BETWEEN THE TOP OF THE TIRE<BR>AND THE INNER FENDER.I ALSO CHOPPED OFF THE<BR>BUMP STOP AND RAISED IT 1" THIS TRANSLATED<BR>TO APPROXIMATLY 2"MORE SUSP.TRAVEL.MY CAR<BR>IS NOW WAAYY LOW!IF THIS IS TOO LOW YOU<BR>CAN PUT SHIMS BETWEEN THE TOP OF THE SPRING<BR>AND THE POCKET.ON THE REAR,CONVERT TO COIL-<BR>-OVER SPRINGS AND JUST DIAL IN THE HEIGHT<BR>YOU WANT!||||reg||6
Z||000009||Monkeyman||10-10-2000||06:41 PM||||Be sure you really want to cut 2" off of your ride height.  That's alot for a car that only has 5.7" of ground clearance to start with.  I hope you don't have any speed bumps (or gravel) where you live.  Add to that, the fact that you might want lower profile tires sometime.  (And it's, "Measure once, cut twice.  Then go buy some more material because you cut too much."  At least that's what I do.)||||reg||1
Z||000010||Xtreme Fiero's||10-10-2000||07:32 PM||||<BR> There are not many speed bumps here where thier are I can get around them easy thare are many low (I mean LOW) sports cars around here I have spoke to a few of them and they get by just fine.<P>Silver eclipse GS: 2" drop<BR>White Celica: can't get a pack of sigs under it<BR>Purpple Escort GT: 3" drop (2 full coils<BR>Blue 89 S-10: 7-10" drop<BR>green 93 S-10: 7-10" drop<BR>black 99 SS S-10 4-4" drop<BR>Green Nissan 3-3 drop"<BR>Black Nissan 4-5" drop (mine  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> )<P>all of thies car/trucks get around here so I think i'll get by just fine.||||reg||1
Z||000011||1FieroGTRacingInc||10-12-2000||12:34 AM||||would a metal blade in a DeWalt saw's'all (sp?) work to cut the spring's??    steve||||reg||1
Z||000012||Xtreme Fiero's||10-12-2000||07:50 AM||||<BR> A dimond tip any thing will work best, you want to keep the springs a cold as posible while you cut. a good cuting wheel will work fastest. But I will take my time with a hack saw and go slow so I don't heat It up ||||reg||1
Z||000013||1FieroGTRacingInc||10-12-2000||11:23 PM||||Xtreme.....   Would the saw's-all with a good *metal* blade work IF I kept WATER POURING ON IT????     Thank's Steve||||reg||6
Z||000014||Xtreme Fiero's||10-12-2000||11:55 PM||||<BR>1FieroGTRaceinc:yeha, just the colder you keep the spring the harder/stiffer it will be and not sag later on. put your spring in a 5=gallon bucket of ice with just the part to be cut sticking out, and use a sharp new blade to cut, after the cut pull out the spring and shove the cut end into the ice and your done  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I will be geting a set of Ground control coil overs for the rear of my car (foud a great price new) I will still be choping the front's though. I'll go with 1.25 coils up front and level it off w/ the coil overs in back  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||1FieroGTRacingInc||10-13-2000||01:23 AM||||Xtreme....   Thank's for the info. I will do the ice in a bucket for sure...  How tough of a job is it to remove the spring's ???<BR>                       Thank's    Steve||||reg||1
Z||000016||Phil||10-13-2000||07:10 AM||||Remember that if you cut coils it will increase your spring rate(makes them stiffer)||||reg||1
Z||000017||Xtreme Fiero's||10-13-2000||07:55 AM||||<BR>1FieroGTR...: It's hard as hell on my car the damn ball joints are STUCK!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I went at it for a wee||||reg||1
Z||000018||MrPBody||10-15-2000||09:30 PM||||I recently cut springs on my '85 4-cyl SE WS6, using an abrasive cutoff disk.  I had no major problems, and the springs didn't get horribly hot.  A few weeks earlier, I installed gas-pressurized shocks and struts which raised ride height probably about 1/2".  To compensate, trying for slightly-lower-than-stock ride height, I cut 1 coil from front and 1/2 coil from rear.  I did not cut more because I want to avoid problems with ground clearance, suspension travel, and lower control arm angulation.  Also, I want to drive the car to work without damaging spine or kidneys.  The resulting ride is harsher for sure, but not unbearable.  I also installed Grand Am wheels on the front to reduce scrub radius, and removed the steering damper.  (Caveat -- do this at your own risk!)  The car seems well-balanced, steers easily, and handles so well I am reluctant to mess with it further.<P>Rodney Dickman's ball joint separator worked great!<P>||||reg||1
